--- (B) DOES the relation lattice carry a graph whose I(.,2) relates to L? ---
 relation-overlap graph on 16 shortest relations:
   vertices=16, edges=89, density=0.742
   independence poly coeffs alpha_k: {0: 1, 1: 16, 2: 31}
   I(G,2) = 157.0   <-- this is the OCF-style partition function of the relation graph
   (compare to L(S) = 0.005102: I(G,2) is a POSITIVE integer >> L, NO direct match)

--- (D) THE SIGN / FLOOR difference (the crux) ---
 H=I(Omega,2)=sum 2^|U| is POSITIVE termwise -> floor 1 (empty set) is STRUCTURAL.
 L=sum prod h has h(t)<0 for t!=0 AND s(t) sign-flips at |t|>=8 -> L is a delicate
 SIGNED cancellation; the level masses Lambda_k GROW (0.11, 0.55, 1.17,...) so there
 is NO termwise floor. That is exactly why inf L>0 is HARD and H>=1 is EASY.

 SIGNED independence sum over the SAME overlap graph (weights = real L contributions):
   sum_{U indep} prod_v relweight(v) = 0.998409
   (main (6/7)^13 added: 1.133210 if 'empty set'=main term)

 CLUSTER-EXPANSION view: L ~ (6/7)^13 * [ 1 + sum signed indep clusters of relations ]
   true L = 0.005102;  theta(support<=4,B=4) = 0.526000
   ratio theta/L = 103.0960  (how well the short-relation lattice sum captures L)

